Article 15 In addition to meeting the conditions for establishment prescribed by laws and administrative regulations, an employing unit that produces occupational disease hazards shall also ensure that its workplaces meet the following occupational health requirements:
(1) The intensity or concentration of occupational disease hazard factors shall comply with the national occupational health standards;
(2) There shall be facilities compatible with the protection against occupational disease hazards;
(3) The production layout shall be reasonable, conforming to the principle of separating hazardous operations from non-hazardous operations;
(4) There shall be supporting sanitary facilities such as changing rooms, washrooms, and rest rooms for pregnant women;
(5) Facilities such as equipment, tools and appliances shall meet the requirements for protecting the physical and mental health of workers;
(6) Other requirements for protecting workers' health as prescribed by laws, administrative regulations and the health administration department of the State Council.

Article 20 Employing units shall adopt the following management measures for the prevention and treatment of occupational diseases:
(1) Set up or designate an occupational health management institution or organization, and equip it with full-time or part-time occupational health management personnel responsible for the prevention and treatment of occupational diseases in the unit;
(2) Formulate plans and implementation schemes for the prevention and treatment of occupational diseases;
(3) Establish and improve occupational health management systems and operating procedures;
(4) Establish and improve occupational health archives and workers' health monitoring archives;
(5) Establish and improve a system for monitoring and assessing occupational disease hazard factors in workplaces;
(6) Establish and improve emergency response plans for occupational disease hazard accidents.

Article 39 Workers shall enjoy the following rights to occupational health protection:
(1) To receive occupational health education and training;
(2) To obtain services for the prevention and treatment of occupational diseases, such as occupational health examinations, diagnosis and treatment of occupational diseases, and rehabilitation;
(3) To know about the occupational disease hazard factors that are present or may be present in the workplace, the consequences of such hazards, and the occupational disease protection measures that should be taken;
(4) To request the employing unit to provide occupational disease protection facilities and personal protective equipment for occupational diseases that meet the requirements for prevention and treatment of occupational diseases, and to improve working conditions;
(5) To criticize, report and accuse acts that violate laws and regulations on prevention and treatment of occupational diseases and that endanger life and health;
(6) To refuse illegal commands and being forced to perform operations without occupational disease protection measures;
(7) To participate in the democratic management of occupational health work in the employing unit, and to put forward opinions and suggestions on the prevention and treatment of occupational diseases.
Employing units shall ensure that workers exercise the rights set out in the preceding paragraph. Any act of reducing a worker's wages, benefits or other treatment, or dissolving or terminating the labor contract concluded with the worker, because the worker has exercised legitimate rights according to law, shall be invalid.

Article 43 Diagnosis of occupational diseases shall be conducted by medical and health institutions that have obtained a "Practicing License for Medical Institutions". The health administration department shall strengthen the standardized management of the diagnosis of occupational diseases, and the specific management measures shall be formulated by the health administration department of the State Council.
Medical and health institutions that undertake the diagnosis of occupational diseases shall also meet the following conditions:
(1) Having medical and health technical personnel appropriate for conducting the diagnosis of occupational diseases;
(2) Having instruments and equipment appropriate for conducting the diagnosis of occupational diseases;
(3) Having a sound quality management system for the diagnosis of occupational diseases.
Medical and health institutions that undertake the diagnosis of occupational diseases shall not refuse a worker's request for diagnosis of an occupational disease.

Article 46 In the diagnosis of an occupational disease, the following factors shall be comprehensively analyzed:
(1) The patient's occupational history;
(2) The history of exposure to occupational disease hazards and the situation of occupational disease hazard factors in the workplace;
(3) Clinical manifestations and auxiliary examination results, among other things.
If there is no evidence to negate the necessary connection between the occupational disease hazard factors and the patient's clinical manifestations, the disease shall be diagnosed as an occupational disease.
The certificate of diagnosis of an occupational disease shall be signed by the attending physicians who participated in the diagnosis and who hold the qualification for diagnosis of occupational diseases, and shall be examined and stamped by the medical and health institution undertaking the diagnosis of occupational diseases.

Article 67 When performing their duties, the health administrative department and its occupational health supervision and law enforcement personnel shall not engage in any of the following acts:
(1) issuing relevant certification documents, qualification certificates or granting approval for construction projects that do not meet the statutory requirements;
(2) failing to perform the duty of supervision and inspection over those that have already obtained relevant certification documents;
(3) upon discovering that an employer has occupational disease hazards that may cause an occupational disease hazard accident, failing to take control measures in a timely manner according to law; or
(4) other acts violating this Law.

Article 69 Where a construction entity violates the provisions of this Law and commits any of the following acts, the health administrative department shall give it a warning and order it to make corrections within a specified time limit; if it fails to make corrections within the time limit, it shall be fined not less than 100,000 yuan but not more than 500,000 yuan; if the circumstances are serious, it shall be ordered to suspend operations that cause occupational disease hazards, or the relevant people's government shall be requested to order the suspension of construction or closure within the limits of authority prescribed by the State Council:
(1) failing to conduct a pre-assessment of occupational disease hazards as required;
(2) failing to submit a pre-assessment report on radioactive occupational disease hazards for a construction project of a medical institution that may generate radioactive occupational disease hazards, or commencing construction without the pre-assessment report on radioactive occupational disease hazards being examined and approved by the health administrative department;
(3) failing to design, construct and put into production and use the occupational disease prevention facilities simultaneously with the main project as required;
(4) the design of occupational disease prevention facilities of a construction project failing to meet the national occupational health standards and hygiene requirements, or commencing construction without the design of prevention facilities of a construction project of a medical institution with serious radioactive occupational disease hazards being examined and approved by the health administrative department;
(5) failing to conduct an assessment of the effect of occupational disease hazard control on occupational disease prevention facilities as required; or
(6) failing to have the occupational disease prevention facilities pass the acceptance inspection as required before the completion of the construction project and its putting into production and use.

Article 70 Where a person violates the provisions of this Law and commits any of the following acts, the health administrative department shall give him a warning and order him to make corrections within a specified time limit; if he fails to make corrections within the time limit, he shall be fined not more than 100,000 yuan:
(1) failing to keep on file, report or publish the results of monitoring and assessment of occupational disease hazard factors in the workplace;
(2) failing to take the occupational disease prevention and control management measures as provided for in Article 20 of this Law;
(3) failing to publish the rules and regulations, operating procedures, and emergency rescue measures for occupational disease hazard accidents relating to the prevention and control of occupational diseases as required;
(4) failing to organize occupational health training for workers as required, or failing to provide guidance and supervision on workers' personal protection against occupational diseases; or
(5) failing to submit toxicological assessment materials and documents of registration or approval for import from relevant authorities as required when using chemical materials related to occupational disease hazards for the first time in China or importing such materials for the first time.

Article 71 Where an employer violates the provisions of this Law and commits any of the following acts, the health administrative department shall order it to make corrections within a specified time limit, give it a warning, and may concurrently impose a fine of not less than 50,000 yuan but not more than 100,000 yuan:
(1) failing to declare the items that cause occupational disease hazards to the health administrative department in a timely and truthful manner as required;
(2) failing to implement daily monitoring of occupational disease hazard factors by designated personnel, or the monitoring system failing to function properly;
(3) failing to inform workers of the true situation of occupational disease hazards when concluding or modifying a labor contract;
(4) failing to organize occupational health examinations, establish occupational health surveillance files, or inform workers in writing of the examination results as required; or
(5) failing to provide copies of occupational health surveillance files to workers when they leave the employer as required by this Law.

Article 72 Where an employer violates the provisions of this Law and commits any of the following acts, the health administrative department shall give it a warning and order it to make corrections within a specified time limit; if it fails to make corrections within the time limit, it shall be fined not less than 50,000 yuan but not more than 200,000 yuan; if the circumstances are serious, it shall be ordered to suspend operations that cause occupational disease hazards, or the relevant people's government shall be requested to order its closure within the limits of authority prescribed by the State Council:
(1) the intensity or concentration of occupational disease hazard factors in the workplace exceeds the national occupational health standards;
(2) failing to provide occupational disease prevention facilities and personal protective equipment against occupational diseases, or the provided occupational disease prevention facilities and personal protective equipment against occupational diseases fail to meet the national occupational health standards and hygiene requirements;
(3) failing to maintain, overhaul or test occupational disease prevention equipment, emergency rescue facilities and personal protective equipment against occupational diseases as required, or failing to keep them in normal operation or usable condition;
(4) failing to monitor and assess occupational disease hazard factors in the workplace as required;
(5) failing to stop operations involving occupational disease hazard factors when, after treatment, the occupational disease hazard factors in the workplace still fail to meet the national occupational health standards and hygiene requirements;
(6) failing to arrange for diagnosis and treatment of occupational disease patients or suspected occupational disease patients as required;
(7) failing to immediately take emergency rescue and control measures or failing to report in a timely manner as required when an acute occupational disease hazard accident occurs or is likely to occur;
(8) failing to set up warning signs and Chinese warning notices in conspicuous positions at workstations where serious occupational disease hazards are generated as required;
(9) refusing the supervision and inspection by the occupational health supervision and administration departments;
(10) concealing, falsifying, altering or destroying relevant materials such as occupational health surveillance files and monitoring and assessment results of occupational disease hazard factors in the workplace, or refusing to provide materials required for occupational disease diagnosis and appraisal; or
(11) failing to bear the expenses for occupational disease diagnosis and appraisal and the medical and living expenses of occupational disease patients as required.

Article 75 Where a person violates the provisions of this Law and falls under any of the following circumstances, the health administrative department shall order him to make rectification within a specified time limit and concurrently impose a fine of not less than 50,000 yuan but not more than 300,000 yuan; if the circumstances are serious, he shall be ordered to suspend operations that cause occupational disease hazards, or the relevant people's government shall be requested to order his closure within the limits of authority prescribed by the State Council:
(1) concealing the occupational disease hazards generated by technologies, processes, equipment or materials and nevertheless adopting them;
(2) concealing the true situation of occupational health of his unit;
(3) toxic or harmful workplaces that may cause acute occupational injuries, radiation workplaces, or the transport and storage of radioactive isotopes failing to comply with the provisions of Article 25 of this Law;
(4) using equipment or materials that may generate occupational disease hazards and are explicitly prohibited by the State;
(5) transferring operations that generate occupational disease hazards to units or individuals that do not have the conditions for occupational disease prevention, or accepting such operations by units or individuals that do not have such conditions;
(6) dismantling or discontinuing the use of occupational disease prevention equipment or emergency rescue facilities without authorization;
(7) assigning workers who have not undergone occupational health examinations, workers with occupational contraindications, minor workers, or female workers during pregnancy or lactation to engage in operations involving exposure to occupational disease hazards or prohibited operations; or
(8) directing workers or compelling them to perform operations without occupational disease prevention measures in violation of rules.
